Overview. The page is working material on genus-3 theta relations, in the same symbolic notation as the previous and next pages. A string of letters, e.g. 'rubc', denotes the theta function with the sum of the corresponding characteristics. Read 'σ' in the transcription as s and 'υ' as v. The page has five blocks.

(A) Top left: squared constants as determinants. Four constants are written as squares of 3×3 determinants:
- (a^{23})^2 = det[[1/(β2'β3'), β2β3, 1], [1/(γ2'γ3'), γ2γ3, 1], [1/(δ2'δ3'), δ2δ3, 1]].
- (a_{23})^2 has the same shape with rows (β2'β3', 1/(β2β3), 1), etc.
- (a_3^2)^2 has rows (β3'/β2', β2/β3, 1), etc.
- (a_2^3)^2 has rows (β2'/β3', β3/β2, 1), etc.

Only the first row is written for the last three. Every row has the shape (x, 1/y, 1) or its dual (1/x, y, 1); Riemann notes these two shapes lower down ('x 1/y 1 (1 y 1/x)'), next to a grid a_i, b_i, c_i with its reciprocals. So these are 3×3 'Vandermonde-like' determinants of a point and its dual. I take them to be formulas expressing squared theta constants, or Göpel/Rosenhain-type moduli, through the quotients α_i, β_i, γ_i, δ_i (my interpretation).

(B) Top right: theta quotients. Riemann defines
- α = θ(rubc)/θ(subc), α' = θ(rvbc)/θ(svbc);
- β = θ(ruac)/θ(suac), β' analogously;
- γ = θ(ruab)/θ(suab);
- δ = αβγ.

These are as on the previous page, with u replaced by v in the primed versions. He then states a Riemann-type theta relation:

αβ' − βα' = θ_0 θ(rsuv) θ(abuv) θ(rsab) / [θ(subc) θ(svac) θ(suac) θ(svbc)] (denominators as implied).

Next, since δδ' = αα'ββ'γγ', he notes γγ' − δδ' = γγ'(1 − αα'ββ'), which is also expressed as a product of thetas. Forming

[√(αβ'/βα') − √(βα'/αβ')] / [√(γγ'/δδ') − √(δδ'/γγ')],

the radicals cancel and the expression equals (αβ' − βα')/(1 − αα'ββ') (my simplification). He writes this as a quotient of four thetas over four thetas. Multiplied by √(ab/cd) it is called k and set equal to a square root of a theta quotient: a modulus expressed by theta constants.

(C) Middle: linear and quadratic relations among products. Linear relations for the pairs (v,w), (u,v), (u,w) are written among the six products pq, pr, ps, rs, qs, qr. For (v,w) the coefficients are
- a^{23}/(α2'α3' a_{23}) for pq;
- (a^{23}/a_{23}) α2α3 for pr;
- a^{23}/a_{23} for ps;
- ... for rs, qs, qr.

The (u,v) and (u,w) lines are only begun. These look like Plücker/Göpel-type linear relations. A quadratic relation labelled 'abc' is begun:

(α3'/α2')(a_3^2/a_2^3) vpq·wrs + (α2 a_3^2)/(α3 a_2^3) vpr·wqs + (a_3^2/a_2^3) vps·wqr + ...

(D) Consistency of the coefficients. Three ratio identities are written, with a_{ij} = a_{ji} and a^{ij} = a^{ji}:
- E1: (a_{23}/a^{23})(a_2^3/a_3^2) = (a_{21}/a^{21})(a_2^1/a_1^2);
- E2: (a_{23}/a^{23})(a_3^2/a_2^3) = (a_{13}/a^{31})(a_3^1/a_1^3);
- E3: (a_{21}/a^{21})(a_1^2/a_2^1) = (a_{31}/a^{31})(a_1^3/a_3^1).

Dividing E1 by E2 and inserting E3 eliminates the lower and upper double-index constants. The result is (a_2^1 a_3^2 a_1^3)^2 / (a_1^2 a_2^3 a_3^1)^2 = 1, which is written as the final boxed result. Earlier, top right, he had already written the unsquared form a_2^1 a_3^2 a_1^3 = a_1^2 a_3^1 a_2^3 and also a_{23} a^{21} a_1^3 = a^{23} a_{21} a_3^1.

(E) Bottom: an elementary Laurent-polynomial problem. Consider

F = (a x + b/y + c)(α/x + β y + γ) − (a'/x + b' y + c')(α' x + β'/y + γ').

Expanding F (my expansion):
- constant term: aα + bβ + cγ − a'α' − b'β' − c'γ';
- xy: aβ − b'α' =: p;
- x: aγ − c'α' =: q;
- 1/y: bγ − c'β' =: r;
- 1/(xy): bα − a'β' = −p', with p' := a'β' − bα;
- 1/x: cα − a'γ' = −q', with q' := a'γ' − cα;
- y: cβ − b'γ' = −r', with r' := b'γ' − cβ.

Riemann solves for everything in terms of α along two chains.
- Chain 1: β' = (bα + p')/a'; then γ = (c'β' + r)/b = c'α/a' + p'c'/(a'b) + r/b; then α' = (aγ − q)/c' = aα/a' + a p'/(a'b) + a r/(b c') − q/c'.
- Chain 2: γ' = (cα + q')/a'; then β = (b'γ' − r')/c = b'α/a' + b'q'/(a'c) − r'/c; then α' = (aβ − p)/b' = aα/a' + a q'/(a'c) − a r'/(b'c) − p/b'.

Equating the two values of α' and dividing by a gives the compatibility condition

q'/(a'c) − r'/(b'c) − p/(ab') = p'/(a'b) + r/(bc') − q/(c'a),

as written on the page. In the homogeneous case p = q = r = p' = q' = r' = 0 he gets
- β' = αb/a', γ' = αc/a';
- γ = αc'/a', β = αb'/a';
- α' = αa/a'.

With these values the constant term also vanishes (my check). Indeed α'x + β'/y + γ' = (α/a')(ax + b/y + c) and α/x + βy + γ = (α/a')(a'/x + b'y + c'), so F ≡ 0. This explains when two such products of a form in (x, 1/y, 1) and a form in the dual variables can coincide; the setting matches the determinants of block (A).
